<title>USA: World beef production seen up 1.5 pct in 1997 - USDA.</title>
<headline>World beef production seen up 1.5 pct in 1997 - USDA.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-10-21</dateline>
<text>
<p>World beef production was projected up just 1.5 percent in 1997 after remaining nearly unchanged in 1996, the U.S. Agriculture Department said Monday.</p>
<p>Production will decrease this year by 7 percent in the EU and 6 percent in the Russian Federation, while it will increase in Brazil, Canada, China and the United States, USDA said in its Livestock and Poultry World Markets and Trade report.</p>
<p>Cattle inventories were expected to fall further in the United States in 1997, after a drought in the first half of 1996, and were also seen lower in Russia, the report said.</p>
<p>The scare over mad cow disease will help push world beef consumption down 1 percent in 1996, followed by a projected further drop of 1 percent to 45.1 million tonnes in 1997.</p>
<p>-- Washington Commodities Desk (202) 898-8394</p>
</text>
